/// \defgroup grp_w1_wwlib_pkey_mpmath WWLib public-key crypto and multi-precision maths
/// \ingroup grp_wwlib_thirdparty
/// \brief Westwood's RSA-style public-key class PKey, the fixed-size big
///        integer Int<64> (BigInt) it is built on, and the XMP_ C routines
///        that do the arithmetic.
///
/// Three layers, all general-purpose WWLib code with no game rules in them:
/// - PKey holds one key (a modulus and an exponent), encrypts and decrypts
///   whole blocks with it, generates key pairs, and converts its numbers to
///   and from DER-encoded integers.
/// - Int, used only as Int<64> (typedef BigInt and bignum), wraps a 2,048-bit
///   two's-complement number and forwards nearly every operator to an XMP_
///   routine.
///   RemainderTable and Generate_Prime, both templates in int.h, find the
///   primes for key generation.
/// - mpmath.cpp holds the XMP_ routines. They work on raw arrays of 32-bit
///   digits and take the digit count as an argument.
///
/// BFME 2's pk.cpp and mpmath.cpp are Zero Hour's WWLib files with the same
/// code. The declaring headers pk.h, int.h and mpmath.h are not in BFME 2's
/// tree: the units compile against the Open-BFME-1 reference copies.
///
/// \par Who uses it
/// No BFME 2 code is known to use it. A scan of the retail image finds no
/// call, jump or stored address that targets PKey::Generate, the
/// DER-decoding PKey constructor, INIClass::Get_PKey, INIClass::Put_PKey,
/// XMP_Rabin_Miller_Test or XMP_Signed_Div, and tools/callers_of.py reports
/// no named caller for any of them. Everything else here is reached only
/// from inside this group or from those two INIClass methods. The one
/// exception is the startup initializer that zeroes Int<64>'s static
/// Remainder. The code is linked into the game but appears to be unused. Zero Hour: only
/// WWLib's own files use PKey: INIClass, and the PKPipe and PKStraw stream
/// stages. BFME 2's ledger has no PKPipe or PKStraw rows.
///
/// \par Modding
/// - Nothing here is data-driven. No game INI field, map script, Apt movie
///   or asset reaches PKey, so there is nothing to configure.
/// - The 2,048-bit ceiling is a compile-time constant (MAX_BIT_PRECISION
///   in mpmath.h and int.h), and the XMP_ routines size their scratch arrays
///   from it.
/// - Nothing in the game calls this code, so changing it cannot affect
///   lockstep sync, CRC checks, saves or replays. That holds as long as the
///   scan above is complete.
/// - INIClass, the only user, is Westwood's own INI reader. It is not the
///   engine's INI loader for game data (see \ref sub_ini).
///
/// \par Remastering
/// - The maths assumes a 32-bit unsigned long. digit is a typedef for
///   unsigned long, and the bit masks and shifts assume 32-bit digits. On a
///   platform where unsigned long is 64 bits (most 64-bit Unix compilers)
///   the routines would not work unchanged. 64-bit Windows keeps it at 32
///   bits.
/// - XMP_Mod_Mult and its helpers read the digit arrays as 16-bit units,
///   which assumes little-endian byte order.
/// - XMP_Prepare_Modulus and XMP_Mod_Mult keep their state in file-scope
///   statics, and Int<64> reports status through static members. None of
///   it is safe to call from two threads at once.
/// - PKey::Encrypt is RSA without padding, and primes pass only trial
///   division and two Fermat bases. A port that needs real security should
///   use a maintained crypto library instead of this code.
/// - Since nothing in the game calls it, a port can leave this code out,
///   with the same caveat about the scan.

/// \file WWVegas/WWLib/int.h
/// \ingroup grp_w1_wwlib_pkey_mpmath
/// \brief The Int big-integer template, the BigInt and bignum typedefs
///        for Int<64>, and the RemainderTable, Gcd and Generate_Prime
///        templates.
///
/// Zero Hour's WWLib INT.H. BFME 2 has no copy in its own tree; its units
/// compile against the Open-BFME-1 reference copy. Everything here is
/// inline or a template, so the code lands in whichever unit uses it. The
/// ledger files the Int<64>, RemainderTable and Generate_Prime copies under
/// pk.cpp, which also defines Int<64>'s static members (Zero Hour defines
/// them in int.cpp, a unit BFME 2 does not have).

/// \file WWVegas/WWLib/mpmath.cpp
/// \ingroup grp_w1_wwlib_pkey_mpmath
/// \brief The XMP_ multi-precision integer routines and the table of small
///        primes they use.
///
/// Zero Hour's mpmath.cpp with the same code. Apart from XMP_Hybrid_Mul and
/// the helpers mp_quo_digit, memrev and pfunc, which are declared only in
/// mpmath.cpp and have C++ linkage, the routines are declared extern "C" in
/// mpmath.h.
///
/// \par Number format
/// A number is an array of digit (unsigned long, 32 bits), least
/// significant digit first, in two's complement: the top bit of the last
/// digit is the sign. The arithmetic routines take the digit count as
/// their last argument, usually called precision (XMP_Hybrid_Mul counts
/// 16-bit units instead). Int<64> always passes 64; XMP_Exponent_Mod
/// works at the modulus's significant length, and XMP_Prepare_Modulus runs
/// some routines on 2-digit numbers. Add, subtract and shift work in place,
/// and Int's operators call them that way. Most multiply and divide
/// routines, and XMP_Exponent_Mod, clear their output before reading their
/// inputs, so there the output must not overlap an input.
///
/// \par What else is here
/// The remaining routines are small and not documented one by one:
/// - setup and moves: XMP_Init, XMP_Move;
/// - bit helpers, inline in mpmath.h with out-of-line copies in retail:
///   XMP_Set_Bit, XMP_Test_Bit, XMP_Bits_To_Mask, XMP_Bits_To_Digits,
///   XMP_Digits_To_Bits, XMP_Is_Negative;
/// - queries: XMP_Count_Bits, XMP_Count_Bytes, XMP_Significance,
///   XMP_Compare and XMP_Test_Eq_Int;
/// - add, subtract, increment, negate and shift: XMP_Add, XMP_Add_Int,
///   XMP_Sub, XMP_Sub_Int, XMP_Inc, XMP_Dec, XMP_Neg, XMP_Abs, XMP_Not,
///   XMP_Rotate_Left, XMP_Shift_Left_Bits, XMP_Shift_Right_Bits;
/// - multiply and divide by a 16-bit number: XMP_Unsigned_Mult_Int,
///   XMP_Signed_Mult_Int, XMP_Unsigned_Div_Int;
/// - signed full multiply: XMP_Signed_Mult, which handles the signs around
///   XMP_Unsigned_Mult;
/// - the double-width product and reduction behind XMP_Mod_Mult:
///   XMP_Double_Mul, XMP_Hybrid_Mul and the quotient estimator
///   mp_quo_digit, plus XMP_Mod_Mult_Clear, which wipes the staged modulus;
/// - byte and text conversion: XMP_Encode, XMP_Encode_Bounded,
///   XMP_Signed_Decode, XMP_Unsigned_Decode, XMP_DER_Encode, XMP_DER_Decode,
///   XMP_DER_Length_Encode and XMP_Decode_ASCII;
/// - random numbers from a Straw: XMP_Randomize, XMP_Randomize_Bounded;
/// - primality: XMP_Is_Prime, XMP_Is_Small_Prime, XMP_Small_Divisors_Test,
///   and the 3,511-entry primeTable (every prime up to 32719) that
///   XMP_Fetch_Prime_Table and XMP_Fetch_Prime_Size expose.
///
/// \par Retail quirks
/// Matched bodies, so retail behaves the same way:
/// - XMP_Encode treats its digit count as a byte count. For Int<64> it
///   scans only the low 64 bytes, so DER encoding drops everything above
///   bit 511. XMP_DER_Decode accepts up to 256 bytes.
/// - XMP_Is_Prime trusts a number once it passes trial division by every
///   table prime and one two-round XMP_Fermat_Test (bases 2 and 3). It
///   never runs XMP_Rabin_Miller_Test.
/// - XMP_Is_Small_Prime passes bsearch the address of its pointer argument
///   instead of the number. Only candidates up to 32719 reach that lookup,
///   so the large numbers PKey works with are not affected.
/// - XMP_Rabin_Miller_Test, which no retail code calls, is close to a
///   Fermat test with random bases rather than a Rabin-Miller test. It
///   copies w - 1 for the exponent before splitting off the power of two,
///   so each base is raised to w - 1 itself; the values it compares as
///   w - 1 hold the odd part; and its squaring loop never updates the value
///   it squares. A round passes when the power is 1 and almost never
///   otherwise.

/// \class PKey
/// \ingroup grp_w1_wwlib_pkey_mpmath
/// \brief One RSA-style key, a modulus and an exponent, that encrypts or
///        decrypts data in fixed-size blocks.
///
/// Keys come in pairs that share a modulus. The fast key's exponent is
/// always 65537 (PKey::Fast_Exponent), so it can be stored as the modulus
/// alone. The slow key's exponent is the inverse of 65537 modulo
/// (p-1)(q-1). Whatever one key encrypts, the other decrypts. PKey::Generate
/// makes a pair. A key is loaded from DER-encoded integers through the
/// constructor or PKey::Decode_Modulus and PKey::Decode_Exponent, and saved
/// with PKey::Encode_Modulus and PKey::Encode_Exponent. INIClass::Put_PKey
/// and INIClass::Get_PKey keep a key in two sections of a Westwood INI
/// file, "PublicKey" for the modulus and "PrivateKey" for the exponent. Each
/// section holds the DER bytes as numbered lines of Base64 text (see
/// INIClass::Put_UUBlock).
///
/// A PKey is a plain value: two Int<64> numbers and the bit precision, with
/// no heap memory and no destructor work. INIClass::Get_PKey returns one by
/// value. The block sizes follow from the precision, which is the
/// modulus's bit count minus one. Only setting the modulus updates it.
/// The data members are public in the header.
///
/// No retail BFME 2 code is known to create or use a PKey. See
/// \ref grp_w1_wwlib_pkey_mpmath.

/// \fn PKey::PKey(void const *exponent, void const *modulus)
/// \brief Builds a key from a DER-encoded exponent and a DER-encoded
///        modulus.
///
/// Decodes both numbers with Int::DERDecode, then sets the bit precision
/// from the modulus. Note the parameter order: the exponent comes first.
/// \param exponent DER INTEGER for the exponent. Not null.
/// \param modulus  DER INTEGER for the modulus. Not null.
/// \note A buffer that is not a DER INTEGER, or holds more than 256 bytes
///       of number, is ignored and that member stays zero. Nothing reports
///       it.
/// \see PKey::Decode_Modulus, PKey::Decode_Exponent

/// \fn static void PKey::Generate(Straw &random, int bits, PKey &fastkey, PKey &slowkey)
/// \brief Generates a matching fast and slow key pair.
///
/// Repeats until a test block survives a round trip:
/// 1. Draws two primes p and q of bits bits each with Generate_Prime,
///    reading random bytes from random.
/// 2. Sets the modulus n = p * q, the fast exponent e = 65537, and the slow
///    exponent d = Int::Inverse of e modulo (p-1)(q-1).
/// 3. Stores n and e in fastkey, n and d in slowkey, and gives both the
///    same bit precision.
/// 4. Fills one plain block with the C runtime's rand(), encrypts it with
///    fastkey, decrypts it with slowkey and compares. A match ends the loop.
///
/// The source's comments give the reason for the test: the primes are only
/// probably prime. They also call generating the primes the longest step.
///
/// \param random  Source of random bytes for the primes.
/// \param bits    Size of each prime in bits. The modulus gets about twice
///                as many.
/// \param fastkey [out] Receives the modulus and the exponent 65537.
/// \param slowkey [out] Receives the modulus and the private exponent.
/// \warning Nothing checks bits. The product must fit in Int<64>'s 2,048
///          bits, and the test block buffers hold 256 bytes.
/// \note The test block comes from rand(), the C runtime's shared
///       generator, so calling this advances that generator.
/// \see Generate_Prime, Int::Inverse, PKey::Encrypt, PKey::Decrypt

/// \fn int PKey::Encrypt(void const *source, int slen, void *dest) const
/// \brief Encrypts whole plain blocks with this key.
///
/// Each block of PKey::Plain_Block_Size bytes is copied into the low bytes
/// of a zeroed Int<64>, so the first byte is the least significant. The
/// number is raised to the key's exponent modulo its modulus with
/// Int::exp_b_mod_c, and the low PKey::Crypt_Block_Size bytes of the result
/// are written to dest. Raw RSA: no padding and no chaining.
///
/// \param source Plain text.
/// \param slen   Bytes available at source. A trailing partial block is
///               ignored and not copied.
/// \param dest   [out] Receives the cipher blocks. It needs
///               PKey::Crypt_Block_Size bytes per whole plain block.
/// \return Bytes written to dest.
/// \warning A cipher block is one byte longer than a plain block. When the
///          modulus's bit count is one more than a multiple of 8 (1,025
///          bits, say), a result can need one bit more than the cipher
///          block holds, and that top bit is lost. Keys from PKey::Generate
///          with a prime size that is a multiple of 8 never have such a
///          modulus.
/// \see PKey::Decrypt, XMP_Exponent_Mod

/// \fn int PKey::Decrypt(void const *source, int slen, void *dest) const
/// \brief Decrypts whole cipher blocks with this key.
///
/// The mirror of PKey::Encrypt: each PKey::Crypt_Block_Size block is read
/// as a little-endian number, raised to the exponent modulo the modulus,
/// and the low PKey::Plain_Block_Size bytes are written to dest. source
/// and dest may be the same buffer, as in PKey::Generate.
///
/// \param source Cipher text.
/// \param slen   Bytes available at source. A trailing partial block is
///               ignored.
/// \param dest   [out] Receives the plain blocks.
/// \return Bytes written to dest.
/// \note A block whose value is not below the modulus makes
///       XMP_Exponent_Mod fail. The output block then holds 1, and only
///       Int<64>'s static Error records the failure.
/// \see PKey::Encrypt

/// \fn int PKey::Encode_Modulus(void *buffer) const
/// \brief Writes the modulus as a DER INTEGER.
///
/// INIClass::Put_PKey is the only caller. It saves the result in the
/// "PublicKey" section.
/// \param buffer [out] Receives the encoding. Null writes nothing.
/// \return Bytes written, or 0 for a null buffer.
/// \warning XMP_Encode keeps only the low 512 bits (see the mpmath.cpp
///          file notes), so a wider modulus is saved truncated.
/// \see PKey::Decode_Modulus, PKey::Encode_Exponent

/// \fn int PKey::Encode_Exponent(void *buffer) const
/// \brief Writes the exponent as a DER INTEGER.
///
/// INIClass::Put_PKey is the only caller. It saves the result in the
/// "PrivateKey" section.
/// \param buffer [out] Receives the encoding. Null writes nothing.
/// \return Bytes written, or 0 for a null buffer.
/// \warning The same 512-bit limit as PKey::Encode_Modulus applies.
/// \see PKey::Decode_Exponent

/// \fn void PKey::Decode_Modulus(void *buffer)
/// \brief Loads the modulus from a DER INTEGER and recomputes the block
///        sizes.
///
/// INIClass::Get_PKey is the only caller. It reads the "PublicKey"
/// section.
/// \param buffer DER INTEGER. Not null. An invalid encoding leaves the
///               modulus unchanged but still recomputes the precision.
/// \see PKey::Encode_Modulus

/// \fn void PKey::Decode_Exponent(void *buffer)
/// \brief Loads the exponent from a DER INTEGER.
///
/// INIClass::Get_PKey is the only caller. For the fast key it DER-encodes
/// 65537 itself and passes that; for the slow key it reads the
/// "PrivateKey" section. The block sizes do not change.
/// \param buffer DER INTEGER. Not null. An invalid encoding leaves the
///               exponent unchanged.
/// \see PKey::Encode_Exponent

/// \fn int PKey::Plain_Block_Size(void) const
/// \brief Bytes of plain text per block: (precision - 1) / 8, rounded
///        down.
///
/// Small enough that every block's value is below the modulus. An inline
/// member of pk.h; retail also has an out-of-line copy that nothing calls.
/// \return The plain block size in bytes.
/// \see PKey::Crypt_Block_Size

/// \fn int PKey::Crypt_Block_Size(void) const
/// \brief Bytes of cipher text per block: PKey::Plain_Block_Size plus 1.
///
/// An inline member of pk.h; retail also has an out-of-line copy that
/// nothing calls.
/// \return The cipher block size in bytes.
/// \see PKey::Encrypt

/// \class Int
/// \ingroup grp_w1_wwlib_pkey_mpmath
/// \brief Fixed-size two's-complement big integer of PRECISION 32-bit
///        digits. BFME 2 uses only Int<64>, a 2,048-bit number (BigInt).
///
/// The value is a public array of digits, least significant first, and an
/// Int converts to a digit pointer, which is how it is passed to the XMP_
/// routines. Nearly every member is a one-line inline wrapper around an
/// XMP_ routine at the class's precision: the operators, comparisons, bit
/// counts, the primality tests, the DER and byte encodings, and the
/// modular exponent and inverse. int.h and mpmath.h both define the size limit,
/// MAX_BIT_PRECISION, as 2,048 bits.
///
/// Status goes through static members that every BigInt shares:
/// Error (the return code of the last XMP_ call that sets it), Carry and
/// Borrow (from the last add or subtract) and Remainder (a scratch number
/// the division and multiplication operators use). That shared state makes
/// the class unsafe to use from two threads.
///
/// Overflow wraps silently. Multiplication keeps the low 2,048 bits, and
/// a result with the top bit set reads as negative.
///
/// \note BFME 2 defines the static members in pk.cpp; Zero Hour defines
///       them in int.cpp.
/// \see PKey, RemainderTable, Generate_Prime

/// \fn Int Int::exp_b_mod_c(const Int &e, const Int &m) const
/// \brief Returns this number raised to e, modulo m.
///
/// Calls XMP_Exponent_Mod and stores its return code in the static
/// Error. PKey::Encrypt and PKey::Decrypt use it, inlined. The out-of-line
/// copy in retail has no caller.
/// \param e The exponent. It and this number must be below m.
/// \param m The modulus. Not zero.
/// \return The result. It is 1 when XMP_Exponent_Mod reports an error.
/// \see XMP_Exponent_Mod

/// \fn Int Int::Inverse(const Int &modulus) const
/// \brief Returns the multiplicative inverse of this number modulo
///        modulus.
///
/// Calls XMP_Inverse_A_Mod_B. PKey::Generate uses it, inlined, to derive
/// the private exponent. The out-of-line copy in retail has no caller.
/// \param modulus The modulus.
/// \return x with (this * x) mod modulus equal to 1. If no inverse exists,
///         the value is meaningless; nothing checks.
/// \see XMP_Inverse_A_Mod_B

/// \class RemainderTable
/// \ingroup grp_w1_wwlib_pkey_mpmath
/// \brief A number's remainders modulo each small prime, updated as the
///        number is stepped, so prime candidates are sieved cheaply.
///
/// A struct template from int.h; BFME 2 instantiates it only for Int<64>,
/// and only Generate_Prime uses it. It holds one 16-bit remainder for each
/// of the 3,511 primes in mpmath.cpp's table (every prime up to 32719) and
/// a flag that says whether any remainder is zero, that is, whether the
/// number has a small prime factor. Each table is about 7 KB, and
/// Generate_Prime keeps two on the stack.
///
/// Computing the table divides the big number once per prime. After that,
/// RemainderTable::Increment keeps it in step with the number using only
/// 16-bit additions, which is what makes the search in Generate_Prime fast.
/// \see Generate_Prime, XMP_Fetch_Prime_Table

/// \fn RemainderTable::RemainderTable(const T &p)
/// \brief Fills the table with p modulo each small prime.
///
/// Uses Int's 16-bit remainder operator, which calls
/// XMP_Unsigned_Div_Int, once per table prime.
/// \param p The starting number.
/// \note The zero-remainder flag starts false and is not computed here.
///       Only RemainderTable::Increment sets it, so Generate_Prime does not
///       sieve its first candidate pair and relies on the full primality
///       test for it.
/// \see RemainderTable::Increment

/// \fn void RemainderTable::Increment(unsigned short increment)
/// \brief Adds increment to the tracked number and updates every remainder
///        and the zero-remainder flag.
///
/// Each entry gets increment added and the prime subtracted until it is
/// back in range. Afterwards the flag is set if any entry is zero.
/// Generate_Prime calls it with 2 for its q and with 4 for its p.
/// \param increment Amount added to the number (defaults to 1 in the
///                  header).
/// \see RemainderTable::RemainderTable

/// \fn template<class T> T Generate_Prime(Straw &rng, int pbits, T const *)
/// \brief Finds a random safe prime p = 2q + 1, with q also prime, that is
///        exactly pbits bits long.
///
/// A function template from int.h; retail has only the Int<64> copy, and
/// PKey::Generate is its only caller. The unnamed pointer argument only
/// selects T; it is never read.
///
/// \par Implementation
/// - q is drawn from rng in the range 2^(pbits-2) to 2^(pbits-1) - 1 and
///   forced odd. p is 2q + 1, so it has exactly pbits bits.
/// - A RemainderTable is built for each. While either table shows a small
///   factor, or either number fails Int::IsPrime (XMP_Is_Prime), q steps
///   by 2 and p by 4, and the tables step with them.
/// - If q passes the top of its range, a new q is drawn.
///
/// \param rng   Source of random bytes, read through Int::Randomize and
///              XMP_Randomize_Bounded.
/// \param pbits Bit length of the prime returned.
/// \return The prime p. Primality is probable, not proven: XMP_Is_Prime
///         uses trial division and Fermat tests to bases 2 and 3.
/// \see RemainderTable, XMP_Is_Prime, PKey::Generate

/// \fn int XMP_Exponent_Mod(digit *expout, const digit *expin, const digit *exponent_ptr, const digit *modulus, int precision)
/// \brief Computes expin raised to exponent_ptr, modulo modulus.
///
/// The core of RSA here: PKey::Encrypt, PKey::Decrypt (through
/// Int::exp_b_mod_c), XMP_Fermat_Test and XMP_Rabin_Miller_Test call it.
///
/// \par Implementation
/// Left-to-right binary exponentiation (the source calls it Russian
/// peasant): after the top exponent bit, each bit squares the running
/// value and, for a 1 bit, multiplies by expin, both with XMP_Mod_Mult.
/// The work runs at the modulus's significant digit count rather than
/// the full precision. XMP_Prepare_Modulus stages the modulus first. At the
/// end it zeroes its local product and calls XMP_Mod_Mult_Clear, which
/// zeroes the staged modulus, the reciprocal and shift statics and the low
/// digits of the double-width staging buffer.
///
/// \param expout       [out] The result. Set to 1 first, so an error
///                     leaves 1 there, and so it must not overlap expin.
/// \param expin        The base. Must be below modulus.
/// \param exponent_ptr The exponent. Must be below modulus.
/// \param modulus      The modulus. Not zero, and at least two significant
///                     digits long (see XMP_Prepare_Modulus).
/// \param precision    Digit count of every argument.
/// \return 0 on success, or: -1 for zero to the power zero, -2 for a zero
///         modulus, -3 if the base is not below the modulus, -4 if the
///         exponent is not below the modulus. -5 is reserved for a modulus
///         XMP_Prepare_Modulus cannot stage, which cannot happen because
///         that function always returns 0. A zero exponent gives 1.
/// \see XMP_Mod_Mult, XMP_Prepare_Modulus

/// \fn int XMP_Prepare_Modulus(const digit *n_modulus, int precision)
/// \brief Stages a modulus for XMP_Mod_Mult.
///
/// Called only by XMP_Exponent_Mod. Copies the modulus and its bit and
/// 16-bit-unit lengths into file-scope statics. It then takes the
/// modulus's leading 32 bits (its top two significant digits, shifted
/// down), computes a scaled reciprocal of them with XMP_Reciprocal and
/// rounds it up. The reciprocal is kept as two 16-bit halves plus a shift
/// count, which mp_quo_digit uses to estimate each quotient digit.
/// \param n_modulus The modulus.
/// \param precision Its digit count.
/// \return Always 0.
/// \warning The modulus needs at least two significant digits. With one,
///          the code reads the word before its copy of the modulus.
/// \warning The staged values are shared statics, so only one modulus can
///          be in use at a time, across all threads.
/// \see XMP_Mod_Mult, XMP_Reciprocal

/// \fn int XMP_Mod_Mult(digit *prod, const digit *multiplicand, const digit *multiplier, int precision)
/// \brief Multiplies two numbers modulo the modulus staged by
///        XMP_Prepare_Modulus.
///
/// Called only by XMP_Exponent_Mod, for every squaring and multiply step.
///
/// \par Implementation
/// XMP_Double_Mul forms the full double-width product in a static staging
/// buffer. The product is then reduced by long division in 16-bit units:
/// mp_quo_digit estimates each quotient unit from the staged reciprocal
/// (the source calls this Smith's modulo reduction), XMP_Hybrid_Mul
/// subtracts that multiple of the modulus, and one subtraction of the
/// modulus corrects an estimate that came out too large, which the source
/// says is rare. The dividend is kept as its one's complement so that the
/// subtraction becomes an addition.
/// The low precision digits that remain are the result.
///
/// \param prod         [out] The product modulo the staged modulus.
/// \param multiplicand Must be below the modulus.
/// \param multiplier   Must be below the modulus.
/// \param precision    The significant digit count of the modulus.
/// \return Always 0.
/// \see XMP_Prepare_Modulus, XMP_Exponent_Mod

/// \fn int XMP_Reciprocal(digit *quotient, const digit *divisor, int precision)
/// \brief Computes a scaled reciprocal of divisor by binary long division.
///
/// With k the divisor's bit count, it starts from a remainder of 2^(k-1),
/// runs k shift-and-subtract steps, and writes the quotient bits from bit k
/// down to bit 1. The result approximates 2^(2k) / divisor. XMP_Prepare_Modulus is the only caller; it passes a
/// 2-digit divisor and shifts the result right by one.
/// \param quotient  [out] The scaled reciprocal.
/// \param divisor   The number to invert. Not zero.
/// \param precision Digit count of both.
/// \return 0, or -1 for a zero divisor.
/// \see XMP_Prepare_Modulus

/// \fn void XMP_Inverse_A_Mod_B(digit *result, digit const *number, digit const *modulus, int precision)
/// \brief Computes the inverse of number modulo modulus with the extended
///        Euclidean algorithm.
///
/// Int::Inverse calls it, and so does PKey::Generate through an inlined
/// copy of Int::Inverse. The remainders and coefficients rotate through
/// three-slot arrays; each step is an XMP_Unsigned_Div and an
/// XMP_Unsigned_Mult. A negative final coefficient has modulus added.
/// \param result    [out] The inverse. It is also scratch space during the
///                  loop, so it must not overlap modulus, which is read
///                  again at the end.
/// \param number    The number to invert.
/// \param modulus   The modulus.
/// \param precision Digit count of all three.
/// \warning It does not check that number and modulus are coprime. If they
///          are not, the result is not an inverse.
/// \see Int::Inverse

/// \fn int XMP_Unsigned_Div(digit *remainder, digit *quotient, digit const *dividend, digit const *divisor, int precision)
/// \brief Divides one unsigned number by another, one bit at a time.
///
/// Called by XMP_Signed_Div and XMP_Inverse_A_Mod_B. Shift-and-subtract
/// long division over the dividend's significant bits.
/// \param remainder [out] Dividend modulo divisor.
/// \param quotient  [out] Dividend divided by divisor, rounded down.
/// \param dividend  The number divided.
/// \param divisor   The number to divide by.
/// \param precision Digit count of all four.
/// \return 0, or -1 for a zero divisor, in which case neither output is
///         written.
/// \note Both outputs are cleared before the dividend is read, so neither
///       may overlap an input.
/// \see XMP_Signed_Div

/// \fn void XMP_Signed_Div(digit *remainder, digit *quotient, digit const *dividend, digit const *divisor, int precision)
/// \brief Divides two signed numbers.
///
/// Copies both operands, takes their absolute values, and calls
/// XMP_Unsigned_Div. If exactly one operand was negative, the quotient is
/// negated and, when the remainder is not zero, lowered by one and the
/// remainder replaced by the divisor's magnitude minus the remainder. The
/// operands are copied first, so the outputs may overlap them.
/// \param remainder [out] Never negative.
/// \param quotient  [out] Rounded down when exactly one operand is
///                  negative.
/// \param dividend  The number divided.
/// \param divisor   The number to divide by.
/// \param precision Digit count of all four.
/// \note A zero divisor is not reported, and the outputs are then
///       meaningless. No retail code calls this function.
/// \see XMP_Unsigned_Div

/// \fn int XMP_Unsigned_Mult(digit *prod, const digit *multiplicand, const digit *multiplier, int precision)
/// \brief Multiplies two unsigned numbers by shift and add.
///
/// Called by XMP_Signed_Mult, which Int's multiplication operators use,
/// and by XMP_Inverse_A_Mod_B. For each bit of the multiplier, from the
/// top, the product is shifted left one bit and the multiplicand added
/// when the bit is 1.
/// \param prod         [out] The product, keeping only the low precision
///                     digits. It is cleared first, so it must not overlap
///                     the inputs.
/// \param multiplicand First factor.
/// \param multiplier   Second factor.
/// \param precision    Digit count of all three.
/// \return Always 0. Overflow is not reported.
/// \see XMP_Mod_Mult

/// \fn bool XMP_Fermat_Test(const digit *candidate_prime, unsigned rounds, int precision)
/// \brief Fermat primality test with the first rounds small primes as
///        bases.
///
/// For each base a from the prime table (2, 3, 5 and so on) it checks that
/// a to the power candidate_prime-1, modulo candidate_prime, is 1, using
/// XMP_Exponent_Mod. The bases are fixed, not random. XMP_Is_Prime is the
/// only caller and asks for two rounds (bases 2 and 3).
/// \param candidate_prime The odd number to test, larger than the bases.
/// \param rounds          Number of bases. Must be below the table size
///                        (an assert in debug builds).
/// \param precision       Digit count of the candidate.
/// \return false if any base proves the number composite, otherwise true
///         (probably prime).
/// \see XMP_Exponent_Mod
